/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `author` DISABLE KEYS */;
INSERT INTO `author` VALUES (26,'J.K. Rowling','British author, philanthropist, film producer, television producer, and screenwriter. Best known for writing the Harry Potter fantasy series, which has sold over 500 million copies worldwide and been translated into 80 languages. She wrote the series under the pen name J.K. Rowling, using her grandmother\'s name as her middle name. The Harry Potter books have spawned a film franchise, theme parks, and a stage play. Rowling has also written crime novels under the pseudonym Robert Galbraith. She is a prominent philanthropist, supporting multiple charities including Lumos and Comic Relief.','https://i.imgur.com/IDVREoC_d.png'),(27,'John Grisham','American novelist, attorney, politician, and activist best known for his popular legal thrillers. His books have been translated into 42 languages and published worldwide. Before becoming a full-time writer, Grisham practiced law for nearly a decade and served in the Mississippi House of Representatives. His first novel, A Time to Kill, was published in 1989, followed by The Firm in 1991 which became a bestseller. Many of his novels have been adapted into successful films, including The Pelican Brief, The Client, and The Runaway Jury. He has sold over 300 million copies worldwide.','https://i.imgur.com/oirf3PL_d.jpeg'),(28,'Dan Brown','American author best known for his thriller novels, including the Robert Langdon series featuring Harvard symbologist Robert Langdon. His books have been translated into 57 languages and sold over 200 million copies worldwide. Brown\'s 2003 novel The Da Vinci Code became an international bestseller, spending over 140 weeks on The New York Times bestseller list. He was a former teacher at Phillips Exeter Academy before turning to writing full-time. His novels blend art history, codes, symbols, religion, and conspiracy theories. Several of his books have been adapted into films starring Tom Hanks.','https://i.imgur.com/D9m0XeF_d.jpeg'),(29,'Haruki Murakami','Japanese writer. His novels, essays, and short stories have been bestsellers in Japan and internationally, translated into 50 languages. He has received numerous awards including the Franz Kafka Prize, the Jerusalem Prize, and the Hans Christian Andersen Literature Award. He has been frequently nominated for the Nobel Prize in Literature. His best-known works include Norwegian Wood, Kafka on the Shore, 1Q84, and The Wind-Up Bird Chronicle. Murakami also translated many works of American literature into Japanese. He owned a jazz bar in Tokyo before becoming a writer.','https://i.imgur.com/FEm86F6_d.jpeg'),(30,'Isaac Asimov','American writer and professor of biochemistry, known for his works of science fiction and popular science. Asimov was one of the \"Big Three\" science fiction writers along with Robert A. Heinlein and Arthur C. Clarke. He wrote or edited more than 500 books and an estimated 90,000 letters and postcards. His most famous works include the Foundation series, the Galactic Empire series, and the Robot series. He coined the term \"robotics\" and developed the Three Laws of Robotics. Asimov was a lifelong member of the humanist movement and served as president of the American Humanist Association.','https://i.imgur.com/cAQegXd_d.jpeg'),(31,'Arthur C. Clarke','British science fiction writer, science writer, futurist, inventor, undersea explorer, and television series host. He co-wrote the screenplay for 2001: A Space Odyssey with Stanley Kubrick, which was based on his short story \"The Sentinel\". Clarke served in the Royal Air Force during World War II and later proposed the concept of geostationary communication satellites. He moved to Sri Lanka in 1956 and lived there until his death. His other notable works include Rendezvous with Rama, The Fountains of Paradise, and the Childhood\'s End. He was knighted by the British government in 1998.','https://i.imgur.com/dy3t6BD_d.jpeg'),(32,'Philip K. Dick','American novelist, short story writer, and essayist whose published work is almost entirely in the science fiction genre. Dick explored philosophical, sociological, and political themes in his works, often focusing on the nature of reality, identity, and mental illness. His short stories have been widely adapted into films, including Blade Runner (based on Do Androids Dream of Electric Sheep?), Total Recall, Minority Report, and The Man in the High Castle (adapted as a television series). He won the Hugo Award for Best Novel in 1963 for The Man in the High Castle.','https://i.imgur.com/inagaZz_d.jpeg'),(33,'Ursula K. Le Guin','American author best known for her works of speculative fiction, including science fiction works and the Earthsea fantasy series. Le Guin won the Hugo Award, Nebula Award, Locus Award, and World Fantasy Award, and was named a Grand Master of the Science Fiction and Fantasy Writers of America. Her works often explored Taoist, anarchist, feminist, and anthropological themes. Notable works include The Left Hand of Darkness, The Dispossessed, and the Earthsea series. She received the National Book Foundation Medal for Distinguished Contribution to American Letters in 2014.','https://i.imgur.com/xpGNhBp_d.jpeg'),(34,'Frank Herbert','American science fiction author best known for the 1965 novel Dune and its five sequels. Herbert\'s Dune series is widely considered one of the greatest works of science fiction ever written, winning the Hugo Award and Nebula Award. He was a journalist and photographer before becoming a novelist. The Dune franchise has expanded to include films, television miniseries, board games, and video games. Herbert explored themes of ecology, politics, religion, and human evolution throughout his works. The original Dune novel has sold over 20 million copies worldwide.','https://i.imgur.com/IaM9vO4_d.jpeg'),(35,'J.R.R. Tolkien','English writer, poet, philologist, and academic, best known as the author of the high fantasy works The Hobbit and The Lord of the Rings. Tolkien served as the Rawlinson and Bosworth Professor of Anglo-Saxon at Oxford University. He was a close friend of C.S. Lewis and a member of the literary discussion group known as the Inklings. His Middle-earth legendarium has influenced countless fantasy writers and inspired major film trilogies directed by Peter Jackson. Tolkien\'s writings on language and mythology have been published posthumously as The Silmarillion and other volumes.','https://i.imgur.com/diQcenL_d.jpeg'),(36,'George R.R. Martin','American novelist and short story writer, screenwriter, and television producer. Best known for his epic fantasy series A Song of Ice and Fire, which was adapted into the HBO series Game of Thrones. Martin worked as a journalist and later as a television writer for The Twilight Zone and Beauty and the Beast. He has won multiple Hugo, Nebula, and Bram Stoker Awards. His works often feature morally complex characters, political intrigue, and unexpected plot twists. Martin also contributes to the Wild Cards shared universe series.','https://i.imgur.com/v4eh5Xh_d.jpeg'),(37,'Terry Pratchett','English humorist, satirist, and author of fantasy novels, best known for his Discworld series of 41 novels. Pratchett was appointed Officer of the Order of the British Empire (OBE) in 1998 and received the World Fantasy Award for Life Achievement in 2010. His books have sold over 85 million copies worldwide and been translated into 37 languages. Pratchett was diagnosed with early-onset Alzheimer\'s disease in 2007 and became a vocal advocate for assisted dying. He was knighted for services to literature in 2009.','https://i.imgur.com/0sJjQvY_d.jpeg'),(38,'Brandon Sanderson','American fantasy and science fiction writer. Best known for the Cosmere universe and finishing Robert Jordan\'s epic fantasy series The Wheel of Time. Sanderson has written the Mistborn series, The Stormlight Archive, and numerous other novels. He was chosen by Jordan\'s widow to complete The Wheel of Time after Jordan\'s death. Sanderson teaches creative writing at Brigham Young University and co-hosts the popular writing podcast Writing Excuses. He has become one of the most commercially successful modern fantasy authors.','https://i.imgur.com/ItDuCZH.jpg'),(39,'Patrick Rothfuss','American writer of epic fantasy. Best known for his planned trilogy The Kingkiller Chronicle, which includes The Name of the Wind and The Wise Man\'s Fear. Rothfuss studied English and worked as a teaching assistant before his debut novel became a bestseller. He has won several awards including the Quill Award and the Alex Award. Rothfuss is known for his slow, meticulous writing process and has been involved in various charitable organizations, including Worldbuilders, which has raised millions of dollars for Heifer International.','https://i.imgur.com/kIFyibP.jpeg'),(40,'Agatha Christie','English writer known for her 66 detective novels and 14 short story collections, particularly those revolving around fictional detectives Hercule Poirot and Miss Marple. Christie is the best-selling fiction writer of all time, with her books having sold over two billion copies worldwide. She wrote the world\'s longest-running play, The Mousetrap, which has been performed in London since 1952. Christie was appointed a Commander of the Order of the British Empire (CBE) in 1956. Her works have been adapted into numerous films, television series, and radio dramas.','https://i.imgur.com/lHm8Dbe.jpeg'),(41,'Arthur Conan Doyle','British writer and physician, most noted for creating the fictional detective Sherlock Holmes. Doyle wrote 56 short stories and four novels featuring Holmes, which are considered milestones in the detective fiction genre. He also wrote science fiction stories, historical novels, plays, poetry, and non-fiction. Doyle was a volunteer physician during the Second Boer War and later wrote a pamphlet defending British involvement. He was knighted in 1902. In later life, Doyle became a spiritualist and wrote about his beliefs in the existence of fairies and ghosts.','https://i.imgur.com/IlgZkGQ.jpg'),(42,'Gillian Flynn','American writer. Flynn has published three novels, which are all thrillers: Sharp Objects, Dark Places, and Gone Girl. She began her career as a journalist for Entertainment Weekly. Gone Girl became a massive bestseller and was adapted into a critically acclaimed film directed by David Fincher. Flynn co-wrote the screenplay for the film adaptation and served as an executive producer. She has won two Edgar Awards for her work. Her novels are known for their dark, twisty plots and complex, morally ambiguous female characters.','https://i.imgur.com/Drihi2d.jpeg'),(43,'Stieg Larsson','Swedish writer, journalist, and activist. Best known for writing the Millennium trilogy of crime novels, which were published posthumously. Larsson was the editor-in-chief of the magazine Expo and a leading expert on right-wing extremism in Sweden. He died of a heart attack in 2004 before any of his novels were published. The Millennium series has sold over 80 million copies worldwide and been adapted into both Swedish and American films. Larsson\'s partner of 30 years, Eva Gabrielsson, has been involved in disputes over his estate and unpublished manuscripts.','https://i.imgur.com/uiOanie.jpeg'),(44,'James Patterson','American author and philanthropist. Among his works are the Alex Cross, Michael Bennett, Women\'s Murder Club, Maximum Ride, Daniel X, NYPD Red, and Private series. Patterson holds the Guinness World Record for the most New York Times bestsellers by a single author. He has sold over 425 million copies of his books worldwide. Patterson is also a philanthropist who has donated millions to independent bookstores, school libraries, and literacy programs. Many of his books are co-written with other authors, and he has been criticized for his prolific ghostwriting practices.','https://i.imgur.com/whtYP6g.jpeg'),(45,'Nora Roberts','American author of more than 225 romance novels. She writes as Nora Roberts for romance novels and as J.D. Robb for the In Death suspense series. Roberts was the first author to be inducted into the Romance Writers of America Hall of Fame. Her books have been translated into 34 languages and have spent over 1,000 weeks on The New York Times bestseller list. She owns a bookstore in Boonsboro, Maryland, and is known for her charitable work. Roberts has sold over 500 million copies of her novels worldwide.','https://i.imgur.com/3wTzgTP.jpeg'),(46,'Nicholas Sparks','American novelist, screenwriter, and film producer. He has published twenty-one novels and two non-fiction books. Sparks\' novels have all been bestsellers, and several have been adapted into films, including The Notebook, A Walk to Remember, Dear John, and The Lucky One. He writes stories set in his home state of North Carolina, often exploring themes of faith, love, tragedy, and redemption. Sparks has donated generously to local schools and founded the Nicholas Sparks Foundation. His books have sold over 115 million copies worldwide.','https://i.imgur.com/NiG5heF.jpeg'),(47,'Jane Austen','English novelist known primarily for her six major novels, which interpret, critique and comment upon the British landed gentry at the end of the 18th century. Austen\'s works are considered classics of English literature and have received widespread critical acclaim. Her novels include Sense and Sensibility, Pride and Prejudice, Mansfield Park, Emma, Northanger Abbey, and Persuasion. Austen\'s works have been adapted into countless films, television series, and stage productions. She wrote anonymously during her lifetime and gained fame only after her death.','https://i.imgur.com/f28hFJW.jpeg'),(48,'Danielle Steel','American writer, best known for her romance novels. She is the bestselling author alive and the fourth bestselling fiction author of all time, with over 800 million copies sold. Steel has written over 190 books, including 141 novels, and holds the Guinness World Record for having at least one book on The New York Times bestseller list for over 400 consecutive weeks. Her novels often explore themes of love, loss, family, and resilience. She writes for four hours every day and has published multiple books in a single year.','https://i.imgur.com/eUJ0Zou.jpeg'),(49,'Julia Quinn','American author of historical romance fiction. Her works have been translated into 41 languages and have appeared on The New York Times Bestseller List 19 times. Quinn is best known for her Bridgerton series, which was adapted into a highly successful Netflix television series produced by Shonda Rhimes. She holds a degree in Art History from Harvard University and a Master\'s in English from the University of Washington. Quinn has won multiple Romance Writers of America RITA Awards.','https://i.imgur.com/GmE9VGq.jpeg'),(50,'H.P. Lovecraft','American writer of weird, science, fantasy, and horror fiction. He is best known for his creation of the Cthulhu Mythos, a shared universe that has inspired countless other writers, filmmakers, and game designers. Lovecraft was largely unknown during his lifetime and died in poverty at age 46. After his death, his works gained cult status and have been published in numerous collections. His philosophy of cosmicism emphasized the insignificance of humanity in a vast, indifferent universe. Lovecraft\'s views on race and class remain controversial.','https://i.imgur.com/CCxNLVh.jpeg'),(51,'Anne Rice','American author of gothic fiction, Christian literature, and erotica. She is best known for her series of novels The Vampire Chronicles, which includes Interview with the Vampire. Rice\'s novels explore themes of immortality, mortality, religion, and morality. She returned to the Catholic Church in 1998 and wrote several novels about the life of Jesus Christ before leaving the church again in 2010. Rice\'s works have been adapted into films, including Interview with the Vampire and Queen of the Damned. She has sold over 100 million copies worldwide.','https://i.imgur.com/O6edMIq.jpeg'),(52,'Clive Barker','English novelist, playwright, author, film director, and visual artist. Barker came to prominence in the mid-1980s with a series of short stories collected in The Books of Blood. He created the Hellraiser and Candyman horror franchises, which have become cult classics. Barker has also written fantasy novels for children and young adults, including The Thief of Always, Abarat, and the Galilee. He is an accomplished painter and illustrator, with his artwork appearing in galleries worldwide. Barker identifies as gay and often includes LGBTQ characters in his works.','https://i.imgur.com/ie83Q98.jpeg'),(53,'Shirley Jackson','American writer, known primarily for her works of horror and mystery. Over the duration of her writing career, which spanned over two decades, Jackson wrote six novels, two memoirs, and over 200 short stories. She is best known for the short story \"The Lottery\" (1948) and the novel The Haunting of Hill House (1959). Her works often explore the dark underbelly of suburban life and the psychological torment of her characters. Jackson suffered from agoraphobia and anxiety disorders, which influenced her writing. She was married to literary critic Stanley Edgar Hyman.','https://i.imgur.com/XPON3zl.jpeg'),(54,'Bram Stoker','Irish author, best known today for his 1897 Gothic horror novel Dracula. Stoker worked as a civil servant and later as the personal assistant of actor Henry Irving. He served as the business manager of the Lyceum Theatre in London for over 27 years. Dracula has been adapted into countless films, television series, and stage productions, becoming one of the most famous monsters in popular culture. Stoker wrote other horror and romance novels, but none achieved the fame of Dracula. He died in poverty after suffering multiple strokes.','https://i.imgur.com/rkTJJCG.jpeg'),(55,'Walter Isaacson','American author, journalist, and professor. He has been the president and CEO of the Aspen Institute, a nonpartisan educational and policy studies organization. Isaacson has written bestselling biographies of Steve Jobs, Albert Einstein, Benjamin Franklin, Henry Kissinger, and Leonardo da Vinci. He also wrote The Innovators, about the digital revolution, and Code Breaker, about CRISPR gene editing. Isaacson served as the chairman of CNN and the editor of TIME magazine. He currently teaches history at Tulane University.','https://i.imgur.com/UjjzxAy.jpeg'),(56,'Ron Chernow','American writer, journalist, and biographer. He has written bestselling and award-winning biographies of historical figures from the world of business, finance, and politics. Chernow won the Pulitzer Prize for Biography and the National Book Award for Washington: A Life (2010). His biography of Alexander Hamilton inspired Lin-Manuel Miranda to create the hit Broadway musical Hamilton. He also wrote The House of Morgan, The Warburgs, Titan (about John D. Rockefeller), and Grant. Chernow is known for his meticulous research and engaging narrative style.','https://i.imgur.com/zjPVPf0.jpeg'),(57,'David McCullough','American author, narrator, popular historian, and lecturer. He is a two-time winner of the Pulitzer Prize and the National Book Award. McCullough wrote bestselling biographies of John Adams, Harry S. Truman, Theodore Roosevelt, and the Brooklyn Bridge. He also wrote histories of the Panama Canal, the Ohio River, and the Wright brothers. McCullough\'s narrative style brought American history to life for millions of readers. He was awarded the Presidential Medal of Freedom, the nation\'s highest civilian honor, in 2006.','https://i.imgur.com/daeoJAm.jpeg'),(58,'Doris Kearns Goodwin','American biographer, historian, and political commentator. She has written biographies of several U.S. presidents, including Lyndon B. Johnson, John F. Kennedy, Franklin D. Roosevelt, and Abraham Lincoln. Goodwin won the Pulitzer Prize for History for No Ordinary Time (1995), about Franklin and Eleanor Roosevelt. She was the first female journalist to enter the Boston Red Sox locker room and has served as a political analyst for NBC News. Goodwin\'s husband was presidential advisor Richard N. Goodwin, who worked for Kennedy and Johnson.','https://i.imgur.com/b4V6LyO.jpeg'),(59,'Jon Meacham','American writer, reviewer, historian and presidential biographer. He is a former executive editor and executive vice president at Random House. Meacham has written bestselling biographies of Thomas Jefferson, Andrew Jackson, George H.W. Bush, and John Lewis. He won the Pulitzer Prize for Biography in 2009 for American Lion: Andrew Jackson in the White House. Meacham regularly appears as a commentator on CNN and NBC News. He teaches at Vanderbilt University and serves on the board of the Council on Foreign Relations.','https://i.imgur.com/49NSN6X.jpeg'),(60,'Howard Zinn','American historian, playwright, and social activist. He was a political science professor at Boston University. Zinn wrote the classic A People\'s History of the United States (1980), which presents American history from the perspective of marginalized groups including Native Americans, enslaved workers, women, and labor activists. The book has sold over two million copies and has been adapted into a documentary and a graphic novel. Zinn was a pilot in World War II and later became a civil rights activist and vocal critic of U.S. foreign policy.','https://i.imgur.com/M7dTpaD.jpeg'),(61,'Barbara Tuchman','American historian and author. She won the Pulitzer Prize twice, for The Guns of August (1962) and Stilwell and the American Experience in China (1971). Tuchman specialized in writing popular history for a general audience, focusing on narrative storytelling rather than academic analysis. Her other notable works include The Proud Tower, A Distant Mirror, and The March of Folly. She was one of the first women to serve as a research assistant at the Institute of Pacific Relations. Tuchman was elected president of the American Academy of Arts and Letters.','https://i.imgur.com/BX6fqJ7.jpg'),(62,'Stephen Ambrose','American historian and biographer of U.S. Presidents Dwight D. Eisenhower and Richard Nixon. He was a longtime professor of history at the University of New Orleans. Ambrose wrote over 30 books, including Band of Brothers, D-Day, Citizen Soldiers, and Undaunted Courage. He co-founded the National World War II Museum in New Orleans. Ambrose came under controversy for alleged plagiarism in several of his books. He was a consultant for the film Saving Private Ryan and the HBO miniseries Band of Brothers.','https://i.imgur.com/ZgAm8xX.jpeg'),(63,'Erik Larson','American journalist and author of historical nonfiction. He has written six national bestsellers, including The Devil in the White City, Thunderstruck, In the Garden of Beasts, Dead Wake, The Splendid and the Vile, and The Demon of Unrest. Larson\'s books blend historical facts with narrative storytelling, often focusing on suspenseful true crime or survival stories. He won the Edgar Award for Best Fact Crime for The Devil in the White City. Larson worked as a journalist for The Wall Street Journal and other publications before becoming a full-time author.','https://i.imgur.com/LnU7f5p.jpeg'),(64,'Simon Schama','English historian specialising in art history, Dutch history, and French history. He is a University Professor of History and Art History at Columbia University. Schama has written over 15 books, including The Embarrassment of Riches, Citizens, Landscape and Memory, and The Power of Art. He is known for his television documentary series for the BBC, including A History of Britain, The Power of Art, and The Story of the Jews. Schama won the National Book Critics Circle Award for Rough Crossings and the CBE in 2018 for services to history.','https://i.imgur.com/0wUkk1u.png'),(65,'Maya Angelou','American poet, memoirist, and civil rights activist. She published seven autobiographies, three books of essays, several books of poetry, and is credited with a list of plays, movies, and television shows spanning over 50 years. Angelou received dozens of awards and over 50 honorary degrees. She is best known for her first autobiography, I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings, which brought her international acclaim. Angelou recited her poem \"On the Pulse of Morning\" at President Bill Clinton\'s inauguration in 1993. She was awarded the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2011.','https://i.imgur.com/GfMY5BR.jpeg'),(66,'Robert Frost','American poet. His work was initially published in England before it was published in the United States. Frost is highly regarded for his realistic depictions of rural life and his command of American colloquial speech. He won the Pulitzer Prize for Poetry four times and served as the poet laureate of Vermont. Frost recited his poem \"The Gift Outright\" at President John F. Kennedy\'s inauguration in 1961. His most famous poems include \"The Road Not Taken,\" \"Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening,\" and \"Mending Wall.\"','https://i.imgur.com/w1hUnvc.jpeg'),(67,'Emily Dickinson','American poet. Dickinson lived much of her life in reclusive isolation. While she was a prolific private poet, fewer than a dozen of her nearly 1,800 poems were published during her lifetime. Her unique style, characterized by short lines, slant rhyme, and unconventional punctuation, was considered eccentric by 19th-century standards. After her death, her family discovered her vast collection of poems, and the first volume was published in 1890. Dickinson is now considered one of the most important figures in American poetry. Her home in Amherst, Massachusetts, is now a museum.','https://i.imgur.com/NvPtKLx_d.jpeg'),(68,'Langston Hughes','American poet, social activist, novelist, playwright, and columnist from Joplin, Missouri. Hughes was a leading figure of the Harlem Renaissance, a cultural movement celebrating African American art, literature, and music in the 1920s and 1930s. He is best known for his jazz poetry, including the collection The Weary Blues. Hughes wrote the poem \"The Negro Speaks of Rivers\" as a teenager and later published the popular character Jesse B. Semple in his columns. He traveled extensively and wrote works in numerous genres.','https://i.imgur.com/cuky6O6_d.jpeg'),(69,'Pablo Neruda','Chilean poet-diplomat and politician who won the Nobel Prize for Literature in 1971. Neruda wrote in a variety of styles, including surrealist, political, and passionate love poems. His best-known works include Twenty Love Poems and a Song of Despair and the epic Canto General. Neruda served as a diplomat in several countries and later as a senator for the Chilean Communist Party. He died shortly after the 1973 coup d\'état that brought Augusto Pinochet to power, and his death is still debated as possible poisoning. His home in Isla Negra is now a museum.','https://i.imgur.com/Qu5RCfw_d.jpeg'),(70,'Dr. Seuss','American children\'s author, political cartoonist, illustrator, poet, animator, screenwriter, and filmmaker. He is best known for his beloved children\'s books, including The Cat in the Hat, Green Eggs and Ham, Horton Hears a Who!, and How the Grinch Stole Christmas! Geisel wrote over 60 books under the pen name Dr. Seuss, which have been translated into over 20 languages and sold over 600 million copies worldwide. He also worked as a political cartoonist during World War II and won the Pulitzer Prize in 1984 for his contribution to children\'s literature.','https://i.imgur.com/iioW7Kp_d.jpeg'),(71,'Roald Dahl','British novelist, short-story writer, poet, screenwriter, and wartime fighter pilot. His books have sold more than 250 million copies worldwide. Dahl is best known for his children\'s novels, including Charlie and the Chocolate Factory, Matilda, James and the Giant Peach, The BFG, Fantastic Mr. Fox, and The Witches. He also wrote darkly humorous adult short stories, many of which were adapted for television series Tales of the Unexpected. Dahl served in the Royal Air Force during World War II and later worked as a spy for British intelligence.','https://i.imgur.com/1uI2JbM_d.jpeg'),(72,'Beatrix Potter','English writer, illustrator, natural scientist, and conservationist. She is best known for her children\'s books featuring animals, such as The Tale of Peter Rabbit, which was her first published work. Potter wrote and illustrated over 60 books throughout her lifetime. She was also a successful sheep farmer and land conservationist, preserving thousands of acres of land in the Lake District for the National Trust. Potter\'s books have sold over 250 million copies worldwide and have been translated into 35 languages. Her life was portrayed in the film Miss Potter.','https://i.imgur.com/EaOVA3O_d.jpeg'),(73,'Maurice Sendak','American illustrator and writer of children\'s books. He became widely known for his book Where the Wild Things Are, published in 1963. Sendak also wrote and illustrated In the Night Kitchen, Outside Over There, and numerous other books. He won the Caldecott Medal for Where the Wild Things Are and the Hans Christian Andersen Award for his body of work. Sendak designed sets and costumes for numerous opera and ballet productions, including The Nutcracker and the opera adaptation of Where the Wild Things Are. He was a gay man who lived with his partner for over 50 years.','https://i.imgur.com/z5OSkFr.jpeg'),(74,'Lewis Carroll','English writer of children\'s fiction, notably Alice\'s Adventures in Wonderland and its sequel Through the Looking-Glass. Carroll was also a mathematician, logician, photographer, and Anglican deacon. His works are characterized by their playful use of language, logic, fantasy, and absurdity. Alice has been adapted into countless films, stage productions, and other media. Carroll was a pioneer of portrait photography, photographing notable figures including the actress Ellen Terry and the poet Alfred, Lord Tennyson. His relationship with the real Alice Liddell and her siblings remains a subject of scholarly debate.','https://i.imgur.com/89p2jtO_d.jpeg');
/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `author` ENABLE KEYS */;
